# A Reproducible Subject-Independent Comparison of Compact CNN, CNN–BiLSTM and CNN–Transformer Models for Single-Channel Sleep Staging

Author names, affiliations and corresponding-author details must be completed by the authors before submission. This neutral journal-style manuscript reports completed experiments; no journal-specific requirements or declarations of author approval are assumed.

## Abstract

{{include:abstract_body.md}}

**Keywords:** automatic sleep staging; Sleep-EDF Expanded; single-channel EEG; CNN–BiLSTM; CNN–Transformer; subject-independent evaluation; reproducible research; paired subject bootstrap.

## 1. Introduction and research motivation

Automatic sleep staging assigns physiological recordings to discrete stages over successive scoring intervals. A useful experimental comparison must preserve the relationship between each EEG epoch, its annotation and the subject from whom it was acquired. Repeated nights and neighboring epochs are dependent observations, so random epoch allocation can obscure generalization to previously unseen subjects. Evaluation choices also change the relative abundance of Wake and sleep stages. These issues motivate a comparison whose eligibility rules and split assignments remain fixed throughout development and final evaluation.

Sleep-EDF Expanded provides public polysomnographic recordings with expert stage annotations [R1–R3]. This study uses its Sleep Cassette cohort and a single Fpz-Cz EEG channel. The research question is whether compact contextual models provide useful performance under one explicitly defined offline protocol, relative to an epoch-level CNN. It is not whether the present architectures constitute new model families or achieve state-of-the-art performance.

N1 merits separate attention because pooled accuracy can conceal errors in a minority class. Independent scorer-reliability research also documents difficulty with N1 [R11]. That literature motivates stage-specific reporting but does not identify the causes of errors in this dataset, whose annotation procedure differs from modern AASM scoring.

## 2. Objectives and study contributions

The objectives were to compare fixed CNN, CNN–BiLSTM and CNN–Transformer configurations; examine predefined weighted-loss references; quantify seed variability separately from subject uncertainty; and preserve all experimental evidence. The study contributes a reproducible comparison, explicit context-boundary handling and a saved-artifact audit, rather than an asserted new architecture.

All final models predict the same original central epochs. Neighbor labels are excluded from inputs, and original indices, recording identities and validity masks remain linked to predictions. Final checkpoint selection precedes test inference. A secondary contribution is documenting class and transition tradeoffs that would be obscured by reporting only the largest test score. The analysis retains every predefined seed; it does not nominate a new test-winning seed.

## 3. Related literature and research gap

DeepSleepNet established raw single-channel convolutional feature extraction with bidirectional temporal recurrence [R4]. SeqSleepNet models sequences hierarchically and produces sequence-level labels [R5]. AttnSleep combines multiresolution feature extraction, recalibration and attention-based temporal modeling [R6]. SleepTransformer applies Transformer modeling with interpretation and uncertainty outputs [R7]. These precedents mean convolution, recurrence, attention and multi-epoch context cannot be presented here as individually novel techniques.

Efficient modeling is also an established objective: TinySleepNet emphasizes a smaller raw-EEG model and training strategy [R8], while DeepSleepNet-Lite studies a simplified contextual model with uncertainty estimates [R9]. U-Sleep addresses a substantially broader setting through heterogeneous cohorts and EEG/EOG inputs [R10]. Those studies motivate examining efficiency and transfer, but they do not provide a protocol-matched numerical leaderboard for the present experiment.

The specific gap addressed here is an auditable, compact-model comparison under one unchanged cohort, channel, subject split and epoch manifest, with all completed seeds and subject-aware uncertainty retained. We do not claim that this focused literature review proves absence of similar comparisons elsewhere. Published results are not ranked numerically against ours: dataset subsets, Wake treatment, scoring conventions, channels, pretraining, cross-validation and subject allocation differ. The models below are compact research implementations, not reproductions of the named published systems.

## 4. Dataset, annotations and quality audit

The local inventory contained {{SC_recordings}} paired Sleep Cassette recordings from {{SC_subjects}} subjects. Sleep Telemetry contained {{ST_recordings}} recordings from {{ST_subjects}} subjects and was inventoried separately; it was not used to train, select or finally evaluate these models. Both Fpz-Cz and Pz-Oz were available in the inventory, but only Fpz-Cz was used. Dataset provenance and the original collection are cited separately from the current PhysioNet platform [R1–R3].

Labels were fixed as Wake=0, N1=1, N2=2, N3=3 and REM=4, with original stages 3 and 4 merged into N3. Movement and unknown annotations received no supervised label. SC exclusions included {{SC_movement_epochs}} movement epochs and {{SC_unknown_epochs}} unknown epochs. The audit recorded {{SC_missing_epochs}} missing complete epochs, {{SC_overlap_epochs}} overlapping epochs and {{SC_invalid_timing_epochs}} invalid-timing epochs in SC. These separate quantities should not be conflated with annotation overhang or excluded labels.

Epochs are half-open intervals `[30k, 30(k+1))` from PSG start. Eligibility requires a verified common time origin, exact sample-grid boundaries and unique complete annotation coverage. No timestamp shift, interpolation or invented beyond-signal epoch was permitted. A documented recording had a supervised annotation extending beyond its signal; only supported in-signal epochs were retained. The historical inventory and exception records remain available for this case rather than silently repairing the annotation.

Raw and filtered signals were checked for nonfinite values, and exact constant raw epochs were subject to rejection. ADC rail contacts and recording-edge proximity were retained as flags rather than converted into new amplitude exclusions. The final test population contains {{flagged_test}} flagged epochs. Such flags describe numerical properties; they neither certify artifact-free EEG nor provide expert artifact diagnoses.

## 5. EEG preprocessing and Wake eligibility

Fpz-Cz was used at its native 100 Hz rate. Calibrated EEG was expressed in microvolts and filtered as a continuous recording before extraction of 3,000-sample epochs. The saved filter was a fourth-order Butterworth prototype with passband specifications 0.3–35 Hz, implemented as `butter(4, [0.3,35], fs=100, btype="bandpass", output="sos")` followed by `sosfiltfilt` in float64 with odd padding of 27 samples. Band-pass transformation gives eighth-order one-pass filtering; forward/backward application squares the amplitude response and gives an effective order of 16. Cutoff gain is approximately −6.02 dB after the two passes. Acquisition filtering and residual boundary effects remain limitations; padding is not exported as physiological samples.

Protocol B retains all valid supervised epochs. Primary Protocol A retains up to 60 original epochs before the first and after the last valid sleep epoch, together with all eligible epochs between them, including internal Wake. This is a 30-minute margin on each side. Original gaps remain gaps. Protocol B contains {{full_epochs}} valid epochs; A contains {{protocol_A_epochs}}, removing {{wake_removed}} exterior Wake epochs. Wake prevalence changes from {{wake_full_pct}}% to {{wake_A_pct}}%. Non-Wake counts do not change.

Filtered, unnormalized float32 epochs were stored once in memory-mappable arrays. Protocol A normalization was fitted only on training samples, using a global population mean of {{norm_mean}} µV and standard deviation of {{norm_std}} µV, with `ddof=0`. The same fixed parameters were applied on loading to validation and test epochs:

$$x'_{k,n}=(x_{k,n}-\mu_{\mathrm{train}})/\sigma_{\mathrm{train}}.$$

Label-based trimming uses information unavailable to an autonomous sleep-boundary detector. Together with noncausal filtering and future EEG context, it defines an offline research evaluation, not a demonstrated streaming system.

