## 6. Architecture details and mathematical formulations

### 6.1. Shared epoch encoder and CNN baseline

The encoder has four bias-free Conv1d blocks with channel widths `(16,32,64,64)`, kernels `(25,9,7,5)` and strides `(2,2,2,1)`, using half-kernel padding. Each block applies GroupNorm with four groups, ReLU and MaxPool1d of width two. Adaptive temporal average pooling produces a 64-dimensional epoch feature `z_k=E(x'_k)`. The baseline M1 applies dropout 0.3 and a linear `64→5` classifier. M1 contains {{parameters_M1}} trainable parameters. M4 has the identical architecture but uses weighted training loss.

The encoder in the context models has the same convolution/pooling construction with its classifier removed. Each run starts from fresh seeded weights; contextual runs do not fine-tune the trained M1 checkpoint. GroupNorm does not accumulate running batch statistics. Its temporal normalization and global pooling mean that local convolutional receptive-field calculations alone do not describe the entire epoch's effective support.

### 6.2. Boundary-safe context

For original index `k`, the contextual input contains valid neighbors from `k−2` through `k+2`. A neighbor must share subject, recording, night and split; all intervening original indices must be eligible and contiguous. Lookup stops at the first gap in either direction. Missing neighbors are zero-filled after normalization and accompanied by an explicit binary mask `m_t`. The central epoch is always present. Masks and original context IDs are preserved with the predictions. Neither neighbor annotations nor predicted stage sequences are inputs.

The packed implementation tensor has five slots, each containing 3,000 EEG samples and a separate final mask scalar. This scalar is unpacked before convolution and is not an additional EEG sample. The nominal contextual window covers five epochs, including up to 60 seconds of future EEG after the central epoch; gaps may shorten available context. No recurrent state persists across windows or recordings.

### 6.3. CNN–BiLSTM, M5

Separate forward and backward LSTM cells have hidden width 32 per direction. For the relevant traversal, candidate states follow the standard gated recurrence:

$$[i_t,f_t,o_t,g_t]=[\sigma,\sigma,\sigma,\tanh](W[z_t;h_{t-1}]+b),$$
$$\widetilde c_t=f_t\odot c_{t-1}+i_t\odot g_t,\qquad \widetilde h_t=o_t\odot\tanh(\widetilde c_t).$$

Masking retains the previous hidden and cell states whenever the current slot is missing. The forward and backward hidden states at the central position are concatenated, normalized by LayerNorm and passed through dropout 0.3 and a linear five-class head. M5 contains {{parameters_M5}} parameters.

### 6.4. CNN–Transformer, M7

Five learned 64-dimensional position vectors are added to the masked EEG tokens. Two context blocks each use four-head self-attention with head width 16, residual connections and LayerNorm, followed by a `64→128→64` feed-forward network with GELU and dropout 0.1. Attention-weight dropout is also 0.1. With missing keys assigned negative-infinite attention logits,

$$A=\operatorname{softmax}(QK^\top/\sqrt{16}+M),\qquad \operatorname{Attn}(Q,K,V)=AV.$$

Each block uses post-residual normalization; padded token outputs are zeroed after the block. Only the central output is classified after final LayerNorm, dropout 0.3 and the `64→5` head. M7 contains {{parameters_M7}} parameters. This is a compact raw-EEG contextual implementation, not a reproduction of SleepTransformer [R7].

![Figure 3. Implemented architecture overview](../results/publication_figures/01_architectures.png)

*Figure 3.* EEG encoding and central-target heads. The baseline differs from context models in both architecture and accessible temporal input; the experiment cannot isolate architecture alone.

## 7. Experimental setup, optimization and losses

The primary comparison uses AdamW with learning rate 0.001, weight decay 0.01, betas `(0.9,0.999)` and epsilon `1e-8`. Batch size is 16, precision float32, maximum training budget 25 epochs, early-stopping patience eight, and no learning-rate scheduler. Best-epoch selection uses strictly highest complete-validation five-class macro-F1. There is no test-based checkpoint update or threshold tuning.

Ordinary cross-entropy is the primary loss. Weighted CE references use training-only counts `(43782,15249,47664,9302,18050)` in the fixed class order and weights

$$w_c=N_{\mathrm{train}}/(5n_c),\qquad L_{\mathrm{WCE}}=\frac{\sum_j w_{y_j}[-\log p_{j,y_j}]}{\sum_j w_{y_j}}.$$

No validation/test label determines a loss weight. Ordinary validation CE is retained separately from objective-specific weighted loss. Earlier bounded optimizer and focal-loss pilots are historical development evidence rather than additional final comparators. Adam and AdamW have different coupled versus decoupled regularization semantics [R12], so matching a numeric weight-decay setting would not isolate identical regularization across optimizers.

Experiments ran on an Apple M4 MacBook Air with 16 GB unified memory, PyTorch MPS and external SSD storage. Existing read-only memory maps, bounded loaders and the established checkpoint manager were reused. Every completed epoch saved optimizer, RNG, sampler, early-stop and history states. Interrupted epochs repeat from a recoverable committed checkpoint. This preserves resumability without promising bitwise-identical MPS results across software or hardware.

## 8. Subject-independent evaluation and validation selection

The frozen allocation used subject seed 42, with all nights of a subject assigned together. Training used {{train_epochs}} epochs from {{train_subjects}} subjects; validation used {{validation_epochs}} epochs from {{validation_subjects}} subjects. Final evaluation used {{test_epochs}} epochs from {{test_subjects}} subjects and {{test_recordings}} recordings. There is no subject or recording overlap between splits.

The initial advanced-model campaign compared architecture/loss configurations using validation results. Its sealed seed-42 ranking shortlisted ordinary-CE M5 and M7, which were then repeated at seeds 43 and 44. The original M1 seed 42 was preserved rather than retrained. Validation macro-F1 means were {{validation_M1_macro}} for M1, {{validation_M5_macro}} for M5 and {{validation_M7_macro}} for M7. The shortlist and best epochs therefore use validation selection and should not be mistaken for unbiased validation estimates.

Before test predictions, the final plan fixed {{selected_checkpoints}} checkpoints: M1 seed 42; M5 and M7 ordinary CE at seeds 42, 43 and 44; M4 seed 42; and weighted M5 seed 42. M4 and weighted M5 are secondary class-imbalance comparisons. The exact model configurations, checkpoint hashes, data/software fingerprints and central prediction rule are recorded in the immutable plan and [checkpoint table](../results/publication_tables/09_all_nine_checkpoints.md). M8 was not included in the final test roster. Explicit local Terminal authorization followed plan review and freezing.

No ensemble was evaluated, and no single seed is now promoted on the basis of its test result. The frozen test has been used. Any further model development or eligibility changes require independent new evaluation data.

## 9. Metrics and subject-aware statistical analysis

Primary macro-F1 is the arithmetic mean of class F1 over all five classes: `F1_c=2TP_c/(2TP_c+FP_c+FN_c)` and `macro-F1=(1/5)∑_c F1_c`. Accuracy uses every retained central target; single-label multiclass micro-F1 equals accuracy. Weighted F1 weights classes by true support. Balanced accuracy averages recall over classes present in ground truth. Cohen's kappa is `(accuracy−p_e)/(1−p_e)`, where `p_e` is chance agreement from the empirical true/predicted marginals. Specificity is `TN/(TN+FP)`. Class precision, recall, F1 and one-vs-rest specificity are reported with support. Undefined class precision/recall/F1 is zero; an absent class remains in five-class macro-F1. Undefined specificity, kappa, ROC AUC or average precision is null rather than invented.

One-vs-rest ROC/PR, multiclass Brier score and top-label calibration results were retained in final artifacts. Brier score is mean summed squared error over the five probabilities. ECE uses ten equal-width confidence bins, weighted by each bin's target count, with the final bin including probability one. No temperature, calibration transform or threshold was fitted to the test set.

Subject uncertainty uses the predefined paired cluster bootstrap with {{bootstrap_draws}} draws and RNG seed 20261016. Each draw samples the test subjects with replacement; every selected subject contributes all of its epochs and nights with multiplicity. Identical draws are reused across models and seeds. Metrics are calculated from pooled weighted confusion counts. For M5/M7, model-level estimates average the metrics from the three fixed fitted seeds, not their probabilities. Differences are paired within each draw.

We report 95% percentile intervals and approximate Bonferroni 98.333% marginal intervals for the three predeclared primary macro-F1 comparisons. Other stage/subset analyses remain descriptive. Undefined kappa replicates are excluded with valid-replicate counts recorded. The bootstrap conditions on these fitted seeds and one frozen split; it does not quantify uncertainty from new training, alternative split selection, scorer variation or population shift. Only {{test_subjects}} independent test subjects are available, limiting interval coverage and precision. Training-seed sample SD uses `ddof=1` and is reported separately; it is undefined for a single seed.

