## 12. Secondary weighted-loss comparisons

M4's final macro-F1 was {{M4_macro_f1}} and N1 F1 {{M4_n1_f1}}, compared with M1's {{test_M1_macro_f1}} and {{test_M1_n1_f1}}. The macro-F1 difference was {{M4_macro_f1_delta}}, with descriptive 95% interval [{{M4_macro_f1_low}}, {{M4_macro_f1_high}}]. Weighted M5 seed 42 achieved macro-F1 {{m5_wce_s42_macro_f1}} and N1 F1 {{m5_wce_s42_n1_f1}}, compared with ordinary-CE M5 seed 42 values {{m5_ce_s42_F1}} and {{m5_ce_s42_N1}}. Its macro-F1 difference interval was [{{m5_wce_s42_macro_f1_low}}, {{m5_wce_s42_macro_f1_high}}].

Both secondary aggregate intervals contain zero. N1 point estimates increase in these matched seed-42 references, but they do not justify promoting weighted loss to a new test-selected primary model. The comparisons have one seed per configuration, and stage-specific intervals are exploratory without an additional multiplicity guarantee. Validation and test directions also need not coincide; neither loss nor checkpoint was revised after observing these outcomes.

## 13. Temporal boundaries, quality subsets and example hypnograms

The transition definition compares each target with the immediately preceding consecutive original epoch within the same subject, recording and night. There are {{transition_epochs}} transition targets and {{stable_epochs}} stable targets; {{excluded_transition_boundary_epochs}} recording-start/gap targets do not enter either subset. Previous true labels are used only for this retrospective analysis, never for model inference.

M1 transition macro-F1 was {{M1_transition_f1}}, compared with fixed-seed means {{M5_transition_f1}} for M5 and {{M7_transition_f1}} for M7. Stable-target means were {{M1_stable_f1}}, {{M5_stable_f1}} and {{M7_stable_f1}}, respectively. These descriptive results qualify the aggregate improvements: contextual models performed better on stable targets but lower on the defined transition subset. Temporal smoothing is a plausible interpretation, not an experimentally isolated cause or a newly optimized mechanism.

Flagged versus flag-free metrics are retained unchanged in [the quality-subset table](../results/publication_tables/12_quality_subsets.md). The small flagged population is class-composition confounded, so favorable accuracy there cannot establish artifact robustness. No flag-based exclusion or threshold was introduced. The displayed hypnogram is the lexicographically first test recording, selected by identifier rather than by favorable performance, with original onset times and gaps preserved.

