## 14. Computational complexity and parameter efficiency

M1 has {{parameters_M1}} parameters, M5 {{parameters_M5}} and M7 {{parameters_M7}}. The analytic partial forward MAC counts per central target are approximately {{M1_MAC_millions}}, {{M5_MAC_millions}} and {{M7_MAC_millions}} million. Contextual counts include re-encoding five epochs for every target, matching the implemented overlapping-window inference rather than an assumed embedding-cache optimization. Counts omit normalization, activations, pooling, softmax, bias additions and elementwise recurrent operations, so they are not complete FLOP estimates.

Mean measured training-attempt durations were {{M1_train_minutes}}, {{M5_train_minutes}} and {{M7_train_minutes}} minutes, while chunk inference/export over the entire test target set took {{M1_inference_seconds}}, {{M5_inference_seconds}} and {{M7_inference_seconds}} mean wall seconds. Training times include the realized early-stopping trajectories and differ in epoch counts; inference/export includes data access, checkpoint loading and disk work within chunks. Neither provides a controlled kernel-latency or energy measurement. Differences across separate executions may reflect thermal state, caches and system load. Serialized checkpoints include optimizer/RNG/metadata and exceed the theoretical float32 parameter storage.

The parameter/cost table and plots describe a tradeoff rather than an efficiency optimum. CNN–BiLSTM uses fewer parameters than CNN–Transformer while producing a close fixed-seed mean macro-F1; the uncertainty does not establish that either architecture dominates on new populations.

![Figure 12. Parameters and measured export cost](../results/publication_figures/09_parameter_cost.png)

*Figure 12.* Saved fixed-seed mean test scores against parameter count and measured wall time. No deployment-energy claim follows from parameter count alone.

## 15. Convergence and exploratory ablations

Validation learning curves are shown for every predefined primary seed. Different realized best epochs and stopping times are part of the saved evidence, rather than reasons to select an epoch using test scores.

![Figure 13. Training convergence](../results/publication_figures/08_training_convergence.png)

*Figure 13.* Validation-only macro-F1; stars mark the checkpoint selected before final evaluation.

Exploratory M8 combines multiscale convolution, center-query attention and gated central/context fusion. Its full-budget CE seed-42 validation macro-F1 was {{m8_ce_s42_macro}}. Fresh three-epoch control, no-multiscale and no-context runs scored {{m8_ablation_control_s42_macro}}, {{m8_ablation_no_multiscale_s42_macro}} and {{m8_ablation_no_context_s42_macro}}. Removing components also changes parameter capacity and computation, and a short one-seed trajectory cannot establish converged module efficacy. There is no gate-only or parameter-matched control. The full-budget result is not an equal-budget substitute for the bounded control.

M8 remains exploratory and was never finally evaluated. Its familiar component combination does not establish architectural novelty. Proposed further validation campaigns were not executed here and must not now be used to iterate against the consumed test cohort. Reliable new model-development claims require new independent evaluation data and a separately registered design.

## 16. Representations and explainability

Saved 64-dimensional embeddings support PCA over all final targets and t-SNE over a uniformly sampled, seeded subset of 500 targets. The existing t-SNE analysis used seed 42, perplexity 30 and 500 iterations [R13]. These parameters were fixed before final reporting. Class, subject, recording, original epoch and checkpoint identities remain linked in the publication coordinate tables. Each model's projection has an independent coordinate system; visual distances cannot be compared as though axes were shared. Projected separation is descriptive, not evidence of causal features or a substitute for held-out classification metrics.

Representative saliency, compatible one-dimensional Grad-CAM and attention plots reuse previously saved validation examples bound to the same selected checkpoints. The N1 illustration is the first saved validation example of that true class, chosen without test outcomes. Gradients target the predicted logit, and attention corresponds to the saved final Transformer block. Their scales and architectural meaning differ across models. No new gradient extraction, model inference or retraining was performed to construct this publication package.

