## 17. Discussion in relation to prior research

The principal finding is that the two contextual compact variants improved aggregate holdout macro-F1 relative to the preserved CNN baseline within this protocol. This agrees with the motivation for temporal modeling in established sleep-staging architectures [R4–R7], but the experiment does not isolate recurrence or attention as the cause: the context models access neighboring EEG that M1 does not. Their nearly overlapping model-level results and the interval for their direct difference argue against selecting a preferred family solely from the test point estimates.

The weighted-loss references illustrate that rare-class and aggregate objectives need not move together, while transition analysis demonstrates that an overall improvement can coexist with poorer boundary-target scores. These are study-specific observations rather than general laws about weighting or context. Earlier N1 scorer-reliability findings [R11] and uncertainty-aware systems [R7,R9] motivate caution, not a claim that present model errors are equivalent to human disagreement.

The study complements efficient and cross-cohort work [R8–R10] by preserving checkpoint and epoch lineage on commodity hardware. However, a small model and reproducible workflow do not by themselves establish an algorithmic novelty contribution. Protocol differences prevent direct performance ranking against those publications. No state-of-the-art, human-level or clinical-effectiveness assertion is made.

## 18. Limitations and threats to validity

The data represent one historical cohort and one frozen subject allocation. The final independent units are subjects, not the much larger number of epochs. Subject-bootstrap coverage may be unstable with a small cohort, especially for rare classes, and is conditional on the saved seeds and selected split. Scorer uncertainty is not directly measured. The class mapping merges original deep-sleep stages and does not recreate modern multi-scorer AASM labels.

M1 has fewer seeds than M5/M7, preventing a fully balanced seed comparison. Validation-selected repeats may favor configurations that happened to perform well on that validation cohort. Architecture, parameter count, future context, repeated epoch encoding and realized training time are not separately randomized factors. The reported intervals cannot eliminate these design confounds.

Label-informed eligibility, noncausal filtering and future EEG prevent a causal online interpretation. Quality flags are not expert artifact ground truth, and no external cohort or Sleep Telemetry performance was evaluated. Illustrative seed-42 confusion matrices and explanations are not representative population uncertainty summaries. Bootstrap intervals do not certify clinical effectiveness or guarantee generalization.

Finally, the test cohort is consumed. Further architectural, optimizer, loss, threshold, ensemble or eligibility development cannot legitimately use it as an untouched confirmation set. New claims require independent data; repeated reporting from unchanged saved artifacts does not restore holdout independence.

## 19. Reproducibility and data/code availability

All previous experiment files, processed arrays, frozen manifests, checkpoints, predictions and embeddings are preserved. The machine-readable final plan binds data, model, source and software fingerprints. Permanent exports include all five logits/probabilities, exact epoch/recording/subject identifiers, quality flags and representations. History is checkpoint-authoritative. The publication tables were recalculated from saved outputs, and the subject-bootstrap weights and intervals reproduced the original registered analysis exactly.

The publication package includes a source/claim number ledger, input/output checksums, figure captions and an independently readable reproducibility checklist. No model was retrained and no final inference was repeated. Source-file hashes provide revision evidence; the project did not have an existing Git commit. Data remain available through PhysioNet under its stated terms [R1]; a public project-code archive URL has not yet been supplied and must be completed before claiming public code availability. The manuscript uses no invented ethics approval, funding, author affiliation or submission declaration. Those administrative statements require author completion and institutional review of applicable requirements.

## 20. Conclusion and future work

Under the fixed offline subject-independent protocol, compact CNN–BiLSTM and CNN–Transformer models achieved higher aggregate final-test macro-F1 than the preserved CNN baseline. The direct contextual-model difference is not established as reliable by the reported interval. N1 errors, transition tradeoffs, unequal seed counts and a small single-cohort holdout remain material limitations.

The scientific contribution is a transparent comparison and reproducibility package for established model families. Future studies could address matched context/capacity controls, balanced seed replication, independent-cohort evaluation, causal preprocessing and scorer uncertainty. Such work requires new evaluation data and is not initiated by this manuscript preparation.

